---
/**
 * Why 2600th: two 2600s. The first console in the house was an Atari 2600; the tone came
 * later, in college. The Atari is a 3D model you can load cartridges into (motion on);
 * otherwise a still of it. The steps tell the story in order.
 */
const CARTS = ['Pitfall!', 'Space Invaders', 'Super Breakout'];
---

<section class="pg-section ab-origin" id="why-2600th" aria-labelledby="why-title" data-atari>
  <p class="sg-eyebrow"><span>01</span> Why 2600th</p>
  <h2 id="why-title">Two 2600s. <em>One handle.</em></h2>
  <div class="ab-origin__grid">
    <figure class="ab-atari">
      <div class="ab-atari__stage" data-atari-stage>
        <img class="ab-atari__still" src="/media/3d/atari-2600-still.webp" width="1280" height="720" alt="An Atari 2600 with its woodgrain front, two joysticks and three cartridges beside it" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <canvas class="ab-atari__canvas" data-atari-canvas aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="ab-atari__deck">
        <div class="ab-atari__carts" role="group" aria-label="Load a cartridge">
          {CARTS.map((title, i) => <button type="button" data-cart={i} aria-pressed="false">{title}</button>)}
        </div>
        <p class="ab-atari__readout" aria-live="polite"><i aria-hidden="true"></i><span data-atari-readout>Pick a cartridge</span></p>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="ab-atari__credit">3D model: <a href="https://sketchfab.com/3d-models/atari-2600-2024d933f6214117a399ad4287ede64d">“Atari 2600”</a> by <a href="https://sketchfab.com/dark_igorek">dark_igorek</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, compressed for the web.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ol class="ab-steps">
      <li>
        <p class="ab-steps__when">Early on</p>
        <div><h3>The first 2600 was a console.</h3><p>I was into computers and gaming from an early age. The first console we owned was an Atari 2600.</p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ab-steps__when">2007–2011</p>
        <div><h3>The second was a tone.</h3><p>In college I got interested in hacking and its history. A documentary walked through the phone phreaks: the Cap’n Crunch whistle that happened to play 2600 Hz, the tone long-distance trunks used to mean “line idle”, the blue boxes that spoke the network’s own routing tones, and Kevin Mitnick, who got much of his access by persuading people rather than breaking code.</p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ab-steps__when">The handle</p>
        <div><h3>The handle stuck.</h3><p>I took 2600th as my handle in college and kept it. It carries both: the console we grew up with and the tone that once ran the phone network. The instinct behind it stayed too: learn how a system actually works, people included, then make it do something new. Today the systems are rendering engines, design tools and AI models. <a href="/#origin">The homepage tells it with sound.</a></p></div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
